# Deploy script parameters. Copy to contracts/.env and fill in.
#
# These are per-deployment operator identities, not protocol parameters, which is
# why they are here and not in src/Config.sol. Never commit real addresses.

# Owner of every contract the script deploys. Not a count, deliberately: this line said
# "seven" through two contracts being added, and the deploy script's own ownership check
# had drifted the same way twice. It now derives the list from the `Deployed` struct, so
# there is no number here to go stale either.
#
# A plain address on purpose: an EOA while building,
# a Safe or TimelockController at go-live, with no contract change in between.
#
# REQUIRED on any chain other than a local anvil, like the four below it. It used to default to
# the deploying key everywhere, which made `OwnerRequired()` unreachable - the deployer is never
# zero - and then skipped the handover, because the script hands over only when the owner differs
# from the deployer. The deploying key kept all nine contracts and every ownership post-condition
# passed, having compared the chain against that same defaulted value. The fallback to the
# deploying key now lives only on the local branch, where it is the intent rather than a capture.
RECOUP_OWNER=0x0000000000000000000000000000000000000000

# Where harvested USDC is routed. Must NOT be the deployer, the owner, or the vault.
# Required on any chain other than a local anvil.
RECOUP_YIELD_RECIPIENT=0x0000000000000000000000000000000000000000

# NAVOracle keeper: posts the daily NAV.
RECOUP_KEEPER=0x0000000000000000000000000000000000000000

# The second key. Confirms NAV moves that exceed the deviation budget, after a delay.
# MUST differ from the keeper: a compromised NAV key is the worst realistic attack on
# the protocol, and one key holding both roles is not two-key control. Both the deploy
# script and the oracle itself reject them being equal.
RECOUP_NAV_CONFIRMER=0x0000000000000000000000000000000000000000

# Protocol fee wallet for the EpochHarvester split (Phase 3).
RECOUP_PROTOCOL_FEE_WALLET=0x0000000000000000000000000000000000000000
# The fast pause (go-live item G4). Holds `pause()` on the vault, the credit manager
# and the lender pool, and NOTHING else - `unpause()` and `setBondDepositsPaused` stay
# owner-only, so a compromised guardian cannot shut the borrower's cure.
#
# MUST differ from RECOUP_OWNER. `setGuardian` refuses `owner()`, and `_assertWiring`
# re-checks the pairing after the handover, which is the first moment the final owner
# is observable on chain.
#
# ZERO IS THE SHIPPED DEFAULT AND IT DISABLES THE ROLE. That is correct while the
# owner is an EOA that can already pause in one transaction. It stops being correct at
# the G2 handover, and this is the trap: `_wire` calls `setGuardian` BEFORE `_handOver`,
# so handing over with this unset leaves a protocol whose fast pause does not exist and
# whose acquisition of one is itself a 48-hour timelock operation. No post-condition can
# catch that - `_assertWiring` asserts the guardian EQUALS this parameter, and zero
# equals zero - so the deploy path refuses it instead, by name, whenever RECOUP_OWNER is
# a contract. The refusal is a DEPLOY-time rule: it is the last moment at which naming a
# guardian is one environment variable rather than a timelocked operation.
#
# Setting this on a protocol that was DEPLOYED without one does not install anything. The
# switchover's own post-condition compares the deployed value against this one on all
# three contracts, so a value the chain does not hold is refused there too.
RECOUP_GUARDIAN=0x0000000000000000000000000000000000000000

# ── The Phase-4 switchover (script/WirePhase4.s.sol) ────────────────────────
#
# By the time this runs the deployment is history, so `WirePhase4` takes the live
# addresses from the environment rather than from a deployment record. Every one is
# required; there is no local fallback, because there is no such thing as a switchover
# on a protocol that was not deployed. `RiskParams` is not among them: the script reads
# it off the manager, so there is one fewer address to mistype.
#
# **Under a timelock, use `--sig "queue()"` and nothing else.** Audit round 20:
# a `forge script` broadcast emits one transaction per external call, so reading the
# emitted call list into a timelock UI produces one scheduled operation per leg - and
# the timelock ships with an open executor set, so once they mature ANY address
# executes them in ANY order. Moving the funding pointer before the pause reopens the
# gap round 19 closed and strands lender principal on a pool that is not yet the loss
# sink. `queue()` schedules the whole switchover as one `scheduleBatch`, which
# `executeBatch` then runs atomically or not at all.
#
#   RECOUP_SWITCHOVER_CONFIRM=RECOUP_WIRE_PHASE_4 \
#     forge script script/WirePhase4.s.sol:WirePhase4 --sig "queue()" --rpc-url base --broadcast
#
#   # 48 hours later, from any address:
#   RECOUP_SWITCHOVER_CONFIRM=RECOUP_WIRE_PHASE_4 \
#     forge script script/WirePhase4.s.sol:WirePhase4 --sig "executeQueued()" --rpc-url base --broadcast
#
#   # and the post-condition on its own, any time, no phrase needed:
#   forge script script/WirePhase4.s.sol:WirePhase4 --sig "assertOnly()" --rpc-url base
#
# `--sig "run()"` broadcasts the legs directly and is correct ONLY while the owner is
# still an EOA. If the owner is the timelock, that is the wrong entry point.
#
# **Export these for the one command, do NOT store them here** - the same rule the
# confirmation phrases below follow, and for a sharper reason than convenience:
# `RECOUP_CUSTODY_ADAPTER` already means something else. `Deploy.s.sol` reads it as a
# *mode string* (`direct`, see the mainnet line below) while `WirePhase4` reads it as
# the *deployed adapter's address*. One name, two meanings, and forge auto-loads this
# file into every `forge script` and `forge test` invocation - so an address written
# here silently changes what a later mainnet deploy selects, and `.env` has broken a
# test in exactly this way once already.
#
#   export RECOUP_TIMELOCK=0x...            # owns everything after the governance flip
#   export RECOUP_NAV_ORACLE=0x...
#   export RECOUP_COLLATERAL_VAULT=0x...
#   export RECOUP_CUSTODY_ADAPTER=0x...     # the ADDRESS here, not `direct`
#   export RECOUP_CREDIT_MANAGER=0x...
#   export RECOUP_LENDER_POOL=0x...
#   export RECOUP_LIQUIDITY_SOURCE=0x...
#   export RECOUP_EPOCH_HARVESTER=0x...
#   export RECOUP_LIQUIDATION_AUCTION=0x...

# ── Confirmation phrases: pass these INLINE, never store them here ──────────
#
# forge loads this file automatically, so a phrase written here is present on every
# single `forge script` invocation - which is precisely the accident the phrase
# exists to prevent. Storing it does not weaken the guard, it removes it.
#
# So pass the still-enabled protocol-deploy phrases on the one command that needs them. The
# ReferralRegistry path remains separately hard-disabled on every non-local chain. Partner
# self-registration fixes the source design, but does not authorize a live replacement:
#
#   RECOUP_TESTNET_CONFIRM=RECOUP_DEPLOY_BASE_SEPOLIA \
#     forge script script/Deploy.s.sol:DeployTestnet --rpc-url base_sepolia --broadcast --verify
#
#   ReferralRegistry public-chain broadcast is disabled in DeployReferral.s.sol and requires a
#   later explicit source change plus fresh operational authorization. There is intentionally no
#   live command here.
#
#   RECOUP_MAINNET_CONFIRM=RECOUP_DEPLOY_BASE_MAINNET RECOUP_CUSTODY_ADAPTER=direct \
#     forge script script/Deploy.s.sol:DeployMainnet --rpc-url base --broadcast --verify

# Networks. `base_sepolia` and `base` are the aliases declared in foundry.toml, used
# as `--rpc-url base_sepolia`.
BASE_SEPOLIA_RPC_URL=https://sepolia.base.org

# Only needed for `--verify`. Must be an **etherscan.io** key: the Etherscan V2
# multichain API serves every explorer in the family from one key, and legacy
# per-explorer keys (BaseScan, Polygonscan, BscScan) are rejected with "Invalid API
# key". Get it from etherscan.io/apis, not basescan.org. This is also the variable
# forge reads natively for `--etherscan-api-key`.
#
# Verified 2026-08-03: a free-tier key works for Base Sepolia (84532) but is refused
# on Base mainnet (8453) with "Free API access is not supported for this chain".
# So `--verify` on the mainnet deploy needs a paid plan, or a non-Etherscan verifier
# (`--verifier sourcify`, or Blockscout via `--verifier-url`). Testnet is unaffected.
ETHERSCAN_API_KEY=

# The deploying key. TESTNET ONLY: this is a throwaway that should never hold
# anything but faucet ETH, which is why a gitignored dotenv is proportionate.
# A mainnet deploy does NOT reuse this pattern - use a hardware account or a
# `cast wallet` keystore and pass --account instead.
PRIVATE_KEY=

# Fork tests (optional; defaults to the public Base endpoint).
RUN_FORK_TESTS=false
BASE_RPC_URL=https://mainnet.base.org
